    },
    "description": "DocumentIndex owns a heap-backed character buffer and a cached word offset table. Its assignment operator currently releases the existing buffer, allocates storage for the replacement text, and then copies the characters. An allocation failure during that copy leaves the object with invalid ownership metadata, while an exception during offset-table reconstruction leaves the buffer and index mutually inconsistent. DocumentIndex is copied by value and may be assigned to objects already referenced by readers, so assignment must provide a strong guarantee: after success all owned state must correspond to the source, and after failure the destination must remain unchanged and usable. Implement the ownership transfer so all fallible allocation and copying happens in a separate replacement object, followed by a commit that exchanges ownership handles without allocation, copying, or user callbacks. Destruction of the displaced state may then occur after the commit, and self-assignment must have no observable effect.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the assignment implementation that preserves DocumentIndex validity and unchanged state when replacement allocation or copying fails.",
    "notes": "copy-and-swap fits building the replacement buffer off to the side and committing via non-failing ownership exchange for the strong guarantee; non-throwing-swap is the near miss covering the commit step."
  },
